@namespace XgFilter_Razor.Components.Internal
@attribute [System.ComponentModel.EditorBrowsable(System.ComponentModel.EditorBrowsableState.Never)]
@using System.Diagnostics.CodeAnalysis
@using System.Text.Json
@using XgFilter_Lib.Filtering
@using XgFilter_Lib.Enums
@using XgFilter_Lib.Patterns
@using BgDataTypes_Lib
@implements IDisposable

@* ── FilterPanel.razor ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   NOT consumer surface. Hosts embed FilterSurface, which owns this panel and
   the interaction wiring; the .Internal namespace + EditorBrowsable(Never)
   are the strongest narrowing the Razor toolchain allows (a true `internal`
   component draws CS0262 — the generator hardcodes `public partial`; spike
   result recorded in INSTRUCTIONS Pitfalls). Consuming it from a host is
   banned outright — including host tests (no FindComponent<FilterPanel>).
   Holds no filter state of its own: the selection, what is applied, and
   what this boot's restoration found all live in the app-scoped FilterSetup
   (halheinrich/backgammon#374, SPEC-filtering.md §4), which outlives this
   panel. The panel renders the owner's current snapshot, turns each gesture
   into one of the owner's operations, and re-renders on each snapshot the
   owner publishes — so a remount shows exactly what was on screen before,
   unapplied edits and invalid input included. Apply and Clear filters are
   the owner's commits; every gate the buttons wear is the snapshot's.
   Information hierarchy: the error-range section is first and always visible;
   each of the other eight facets is its own collapsible row, one tier, in the
   lib's FilterFacet declaration order, and all eight rows sit behind one
   further disclosure — the container whose toggle names itself from
   MoreFiltersFoldedLabel / MoreFiltersExpandedLabel — so the panel at rest is
   the error range and the two buttons. That container reads as the rows'
   parent rather than as a ninth row: it carries header weight, and its region
   indents the rows under it. A collapsed row names its facet and, while that
   facet holds an active filter, carries a badge; the folded container carries
   the count of those — see the facet-row and container comments for how
   presence and wording divide.
   What persists, and where, is FilterStorage's: the committed selection
   (the owner writes it, and reads it back once per boot), the set of
   expanded rows and whether the container is open (this panel's display
   preferences, read at each mount and written at each toggle). Every storage
   call goes through that one seam, which makes every requested call and
   tells the host's refusal sink of each refusal; a refused read is nothing
   stored, a refused write leaves the in-memory state standing. A stored
   selection the owner cannot read restores to defaults and says so
   (#filterRestoreFailedNotice): a failed restore is never silent.
   Location: XgFilter_Razor/Components/FilterPanel.razor
──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── *@

@* The panel's root carries the restoration marker (RestorationAttribute,
   halheinrich/backgammon#346): what a host's browser test waits on and reads
   instead of the panel's storage keys. *@
<div class="card mb-4" style="max-width:800px" @attributes="RestorationMarker">
    <div class="card-header">
        <strong>Filters</strong>
    </div>
    <div class="card-body">

        @* ── Restored-selection notice (§4 legibility) ───────────────────────
           A reload ends the setup: selections are restored, applied-ness is
           not, Apply re-arms — correct by rule, but indistinguishable from a
           bug unless the screen says what happened. Shown while the owner says
           so: this boot's restoration put a stored selection on screen and no
           gesture has made it the user's own since; it survives navigation
           with the state it describes and ends at the first edit, load or
           commit — or when the user closes it, which is the same end. A polite
           outcome, not an error.

           An event notice, so dismissible (the umbrella's SPEC-notices.md), and
           its occurrence — this boot's restore — outlives this component, so
           the dismissal is the app-scoped owner's: bound to FilterSetup, which
           is why a navigate-back cannot bring a closed notice back. No @if
           around it and no bit here — the snapshot's flag is the one fact,
           read once; the component renders nothing while it is false, whether
           the notice was never shown or is over. *@
        <Notice id="filterRestoredNotice" class="mb-3"
                Kind="NoticeKind.Information" Announcement="NoticeAnnouncement.Polite"
                Dismissible="true"
                @bind-Dismissed:get="!_snapshot.IsRestoredNoticeShowing"
                @bind-Dismissed:set="_ => Setup.DismissRestoredNotice()">
            This filter was restored from a previous session — it isn't in
            effect until you press <strong>Apply Filter</strong>.
        </Notice>

        @* ── Failed-restore notice ────────────────────────────────────────────
           The restore's other outcome (halheinrich/backgammon#367): a stored
           selection the owner cannot read restores to defaults, and defaults
           with no word are indistinguishable from a first visit — the user's
           filter gone for no stated reason. So the panel says so. Only for a
           document that is present and unreadable: nothing stored is nothing
           failed, and a read the browser refused is the storage condition,
           reported to the host's refusal sink instead.

           An event notice — this boot's restore failed once — so dismissible
           (the umbrella's SPEC-notices.md), held by the same owner for the same
           reason as the box above, and bound the same way — no @if, no bit
           here. It ends when the user closes it or a commit's write lands,
           replacing the unreadable document; an edit leaves it standing, and
           so does a commit whose write the browser refused, since neither
           changes what is stored. Polite: an outcome to understand, not a
           gesture that failed. *@
        <Notice id="filterRestoreFailedNotice" class="mb-3"
                Kind="NoticeKind.Warning" Announcement="NoticeAnnouncement.Polite"
                Dismissible="true"
                @bind-Dismissed:get="!_snapshot.IsFailureNoticeShowing"
                @bind-Dismissed:set="_ => Setup.DismissFailureNotice()">
            Your remembered filter couldn't be read, so the filters start from
            their defaults.
        </Notice>

        @* ── Error range ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
           First and always visible: dogfooding showed it is the panel's
           most-used control, so it never rides behind a row of its own.

           Validity is the lib's ruling, not this panel's: GetInvalidFields()
           names the offending bounds (non-negative, min ≤ max, NaN rejected)
           and each input reds itself on its own FilterField, so a wrong max
           never marks a min the user got right — the lib's attribution rules
           decide that, and a misordered pair blames both by design. Hoisted
           into a local like the active-facet set below: every marked input
           and every feedback line on the panel — this facet's, the score
           tokens', the move numbers', the pattern's — reads that one
           evaluation. The panel writes no rule of its own here — only the
           words for it.

           What a box SHOWS of the verdict is a second fact beside validity
           (halheinrich/backgammon#272, SPEC-filtering.md §1). Validity is
           continuous: the Apply gate reads the owner's verdict on every
           keystroke. The mark and its message for a newly typed error appear
           when the user leaves the box, and clear as soon as the value is
           corrected; a wrong value that was not typed here — restored, staged
           from a saved filter, or still on screen when this panel mounts —
           shows at once. So typing never makes a message appear under the
           box, which is what kept moving the layout beneath the caret; and a
           corrected message's box stays where it was, saying nothing, so a
           correction does not move it either (_heldLines).
           shownFields is that second fact: the wrong fields this panel has
           revealed (ShownFields).

           How an input wears the verdict is rendered from one place too
           (FieldAttributes, halheinrich/backgammon#270): the is-invalid class,
           aria-invalid, and an aria-describedby that names the field's hint
           always and its rendered feedback line only while that line exists.
           Every box on the panel that bears a verdict splats that one result,
           so a seventh field cannot carry the class and miss the
           announcement. *@
        @{ var shownFields = ShownFields; }
        <div class="mb-3">
            @* The heading and its hint carry ids of their own because the two
               boxes below name themselves by reference off them
               (halheinrich/backgammon#196), exactly as a row's controls name
               themselves off the row's toggle and hint. The id is on the
               heading WORDS rather than on the label element around them: a
               reference to the whole label would pull the hint into the name
               and make "(inclusive; blank = no bound)" part of what the box is
               called, which is the lie the position-pattern field's own
               comment records dropping. A `for`-label per input was the other
               way offered and is refused for the same reason it was there:
               one label cannot serve two boxes, and splitting it into visible
               Min / Max labels would redesign the row to say what the
               placeholders already say. The heading itself is the lib's label
               via ToLabel() now that it is what a screen reader reads out —
               the rows' rule, which this section was the last literal to keep
               outside. *@
            <label class="form-label">
                <span id="errorRangeLabel">@FilterFacet.ErrorRange.ToLabel()</span>
                <small class="text-muted" id="errorRangeHint">(inclusive; blank = no bound)</small>
            </label>
            @* Both range facets carry the same Min/Max placeholders, so the
               boxes carry ids: a handle that says which facet it belongs to,
               rather than one that resolves by whichever section renders
               first.

               Text boxes, not number controls (halheinrich/backgammon#374,
               halheinrich/backgammon#379). A browser's number control hands the
               page an empty value for text it cannot read yet — "1e-", "-",
               or a decimal comma in a locale that writes a point — so the draft
               would record no bound while the user is still typing one, and the
               selection could read as the empty one. A text box hands over what
               is typed, and the draft keeps it; what it means as a number is
               the draft's reading (FilterDraft), which accepts either decimal
               mark. inputmode asks a touch keyboard for digits: decimal here,
               where a fraction is the common case, and numeric for the move
               numbers, which are whole. *@
            <div class="d-flex gap-2 align-items-center">
                <input id="errorMin"
                       @attributes="@(FieldAttributes(shownFields, FilterField.ErrorMin, "errorRangeHint", "errorRangeFeedback"))"
                       style="max-width:140px"
                       aria-labelledby="errorRangeLabel"
                       type="text" inputmode="decimal" placeholder="Min"
                       value="@Draft.ErrorMinText"
                       @oninput="e => Edit(d => d with { ErrorMinText = e.Value?.ToString() ?? string.Empty })"
                       @onblur="() => LeaveField(FilterField.ErrorMin)" />
                <span>–</span>
                <input id="errorMax"
                       @attributes="@(FieldAttributes(shownFields, FilterField.ErrorMax, "errorRangeHint", "errorRangeFeedback"))"
                       style="max-width:140px"
                       aria-labelledby="errorRangeLabel"
                       type="text" inputmode="decimal" placeholder="Max"
                       value="@Draft.ErrorMaxText"
                       @oninput="e => Edit(d => d with { ErrorMaxText = e.Value?.ToString() ?? string.Empty })"
                       @onblur="() => LeaveField(FilterField.ErrorMax)" />
            </div>
            @* Shown only while it applies, the #applyDisabledReason idiom,
               rather than left in the DOM for Bootstrap's sibling selector to
               reveal: the inputs sit one level down inside the flex row, so
               `.is-invalid ~ .invalid-feedback` cannot reach across — hence the
               explicit d-block and the @if. Since halheinrich/backgammon#270
               every feedback line on the panel renders this way, because the
               input's aria-describedby names it while it is invalid and a
               reference to a hidden element is read out regardless — so the
               id, the one thing that reference names, exists exactly while
               the line is shown.
               Once shown, the line's box stays where it is
               (halheinrich/backgammon#272): corrected, the message and its
               announcement go at once, but the box keeps its place and size,
               saying nothing, so the layout below does not move while the
               user types. FeedbackLineAttributes renders the two states and
               _heldLines says how long the second lasts — here, the always-
               visible error range, until the panel unmounts.
               One line for both violations, because the
               user fixes them at the same two boxes: "a number, zero or
               greater" is also the true verdict on text that is not a number
               yet — unfinished input such as "1e-", or a word like NaN.
               Keyed to this facet's own two fields, never to "the lib named
               something": the invalid set spans facets (the score tokens
               joined it in halheinrich/backgammon#121), and a line that fired
               on any membership would explain the error bounds over a fault
               the user committed three sections down. *@
            @if (IsLineRendered(shownFields, FilterFacet.ErrorRange))
            {
                <div @attributes="@(FeedbackLineAttributes(IsLineShown(shownFields, FilterFacet.ErrorRange), "errorRangeFeedback"))">
                    Each bound must be a number, zero or greater, and Min can't be
                    above Max — fix it to apply, or clear a bound for no limit on
                    that side.
                </div>
            }
        </div>

        @* ── Facet rows ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
           One collapsible row per offered facet, one tier, inside the More
           filters container below and in the lib's FilterFacet declaration
           order. Each is an honest disclosure in the level-group idiom one
           tier up: a real button carrying aria-expanded / aria-controls over
           the always-rendered #facet_<Facet> region, whose children render
           only while expanded (absent from the DOM when collapsed, not styled
           away), plus a decorative +/− glyph that is aria-hidden because the
           button's own aria-expanded already says which way the row sits. The
           button's accessible name is the facet's own label — the lib's
           FilterFacet [Description] via ToLabel(), never a literal here.
           Every id here is built from the facet's enum member name
           (facetToggle_, facet_, facetBadge_, and facetHint_ where a row's
           hint is referenced), one mould, so a control inside a row can name
           itself by pointing at its header instead of repeating the heading —
           which is exactly what the position-pattern field does.

           The badge divides in two, deliberately. Its PRESENCE is the lib's
           ruling: a row badges exactly when GetActiveFacets() names its facet,
           asked of the draft's parsed config — the config Apply commits —
           never by re-inspecting a box or a config field here: the activation
           predicates are the lib's SSOT, and reading a box directly would be a
           second encoding of one of them. Reading the live draft is also what
           keeps it honest at rest (after restore, Apply, Clear filters and a
           saved filter's staging) and live mid-edit. Its WORDS are the panel's display concern and nothing
           else's — see FacetBadgeText. It renders only while the row is
           collapsed: expanded, the controls themselves say everything the
           badge could, which is the level groups' ruling one tier down.

           Which rows are open is the user's choice, not the config's: the set
           persists under FilterStorage.DisclosureKey, and staging a saved
           filter or Clear filters never moves it. Opening or closing a row is
           navigation, not an edit — the owner hears nothing. The container below keeps its own open state the
           same way, under its own key; a row the user left open is still open
           when the container is next opened, whether or not the container was
           folded over it in between.

           Spacing is the row's, and it belongs to the BODY rather than to the
           row (halheinrich/backgammon#239). The rows pack tightly against one
           another — a stack of one-line headers reads as one list, and the
           blank line the wrapper's mb-3 used to draw between them made eight
           siblings look like eight sections. What still needs a gap beneath it
           is an expanded body, so the next row's header does not sit against
           the controls above it; so the step rides on the #facet_<Facet>
           region and only while that region has children. Conditional, not
           unconditional: the region is rendered in both states, and a margin
           on the empty one would put the blank line straight back between
           collapsed rows. One step throughout — mb-3, the panel's own. *@
        @{
            var activeFacets = ActiveFacets;

            RenderFragment FacetRow(FilterFacet facet, RenderFragment body)
            {
                var expanded = _expandedFacets.Contains(facet);
                var badge = !expanded && activeFacets.Contains(facet)
                    ? FacetBadgeText(facet)
                    : null;

                return @<div>
                    <button type="button" id="facetToggle_@facet"
                            class="btn btn-link btn-sm p-0 text-decoration-none"
                            aria-expanded="@(expanded ? "true" : "false")"
                            aria-controls="facet_@facet"
                            @onclick="() => ToggleFacetAsync(facet)">
                        <span aria-hidden="true">@(expanded ? "−" : "+")</span>
                        @facet.ToLabel()
                        @if (badge is not null)
                        {
                            <span id="facetBadge_@facet" class="badge text-bg-primary ms-1">@badge</span>
                        }
                    </button>
                    <div id="facet_@facet" class="@(expanded ? "mb-3" : null)">
                        @if (expanded)
                        {
                            @body
                        }
                    </div>
                </div>;
            }
        }

        @* ── More / Fewer filters ─────────────────────────────────────────────
           One disclosure over all eight rows (halheinrich/backgammon#231), so
           the panel at rest is the error range and the two buttons — the two
           things a returning user reaches for — and everything else is one
           click away. The error range and the Apply/Clear row stay outside it:
           they are what the fold is measured against.

           The rows' own idiom, one tier up: a real button carrying
           aria-expanded / aria-controls over the always-rendered #moreFilters
           region, whose children render only while expanded (absent from the
           DOM when folded, not styled away), plus the same aria-hidden +/−
           glyph. Its ids are the rows' mould without the per-facet suffix a
           singleton has no use for — moreFiltersToggle, moreFilters,
           moreFiltersBadge — and deliberately NOT facetToggle_/facet_/
           facetBadge_ shaped: those prefixes are surveyed as "the rows", here
           and in both hosts, and a container answering to them would
           misreport itself as a ninth row.

           Folded is the resting state, the posture the hierarchy ruling asks
           for one tier down (every row starts collapsed) and the whole reason
           this container exists: a panel that folded only after a click would
           not fold. What folding hides, the badge says — the number of rows
           whose facet is set, counted off the same ActiveFacets ruling the
           rows' own badges read (the draft's ActiveFacets, the lib's
           GetActiveFacets on its parsed config), never a second count of this
           panel's own. ErrorRange can
           be active and is never counted here: it is not behind this fold.
           Like a row's, the badge speaks only while folded — open, the rows'
           own badges say it in more detail.

           Its open state is the user's preference and persists like the rows',
           but under its own key (MoreFiltersKey) rather than joining the
           DisclosureKey set: that set's vocabulary is FilterFacet member names
           and this container is not a facet, so a sentinel name in it would be
           exactly the corruption its all-or-nothing restore exists to refuse.
           Staging a saved filter and Clear filters never move it, as with the
           rows — opening or closing it is navigation, not an edit, so the
           owner hears nothing.

           Its hierarchy is drawn, not merely meant (halheinrich/backgammon#239).
           Drawn flat in the candidate the user rejected — nothing shipped that
           way — this control was measured expanded at the rows' own
           left edge, size, weight and colour, one 24px rhythm with no gap: the
           thing that folds the panel read as the first of nine identical links,
           told apart only by its glyph. Two marks fix that and both are
           structural rather than decorative. The REGION indents its rows as a
           group — ms-3, the same step a row's own body already takes under its
           header, so the panel says "inside" one way at both tiers rather than
           inventing a second. The TOGGLE takes header weight: fw-bold, the
           card header's <strong> in utility form, and it drops btn-sm, which
           was the rows' tier borrowed — the small scale is what marks the
           repeated tier, and the control over that tier is not of it. That
           lands it at the card body's own font size, bold, a tier above the
           14px regular of the rows it governs. No colour is added: btn-link's
           blue already says "activates", and a second colour here would be a
           second meaning to learn. It stays a button, not a heading element:
           what outline this panel sits in is the host's to know (FilterHelp
           takes a HeadingLevel for exactly that reason) and a hard-coded <h_>
           here would malform an outline this component cannot see.

           The gap after the toggle is the region's too, and conditional for
           the rows' reason one tier down: mt-3 while it has children, nothing
           while it is folded, so the resting panel keeps the tight line
           between this control and the buttons below.

           Its NAME flips with the fold — MoreFiltersFoldedLabel while folded,
           MoreFiltersExpandedLabel while open — so the control says what the
           next click does rather than describing a state the user is already
           looking at. The two words and the rule that picks between them live
           together on this panel and nowhere else: the markup renders
           MoreFiltersLabel, FilterHelp renders the pair, and neither retypes
           either — the same one-owner rule the row headers keep by rendering
           ToLabel(). The accessible name follows the visible label because it
           IS the visible label: the button's own text, with only the glyph
           held out of it as aria-hidden. *@
        @{ var setRowCount = RowFacets.Count(activeFacets.Contains); }
        <div class="mb-3">
            <button type="button" id="moreFiltersToggle"
                    class="btn btn-link p-0 text-decoration-none fw-bold"
                    aria-expanded="@(_moreFiltersExpanded ? "true" : "false")"
                    aria-controls="moreFilters"
                    @onclick="ToggleMoreFiltersAsync">
                <span aria-hidden="true">@(_moreFiltersExpanded ? "−" : "+")</span>
                @MoreFiltersLabel
                @if (!_moreFiltersExpanded && setRowCount > 0)
                {
                    <span id="moreFiltersBadge" class="badge text-bg-primary ms-1">@setRowCount set</span>
                }
            </button>
            <div id="moreFilters" class="ms-3 @(_moreFiltersExpanded ? "mt-3" : string.Empty)">
                @if (_moreFiltersExpanded)
                {
                    @* ── Player ──────────────────────────────────────────────── *@
                    @FacetRow(FilterFacet.Players, @<div class="ms-3">
                        <label class="form-label">
                            <small class="text-muted" id="facetHint_Players">(comma-separated; blank = all players)</small>
                        </label>
                        <input class="form-control"
                               aria-labelledby="facetToggle_Players"
                               aria-describedby="facetHint_Players"
                               placeholder="e.g. Hal, Magriel"
                               value="@Draft.PlayersText"
                               @oninput="e => Edit(d => d with { PlayersText = e.Value?.ToString() ?? string.Empty })" />
                    </div>)

                    @* ── Decision type ───────────────────────────────────────
                       The one offered facet with no bracketed hint: its three options say
                       everything a hint would, so the row's body carries the controls
                       alone rather than an empty label element. *@
                    @FacetRow(FilterFacet.DecisionType, @<div class="ms-3">
                        @foreach (var opt in Enum.GetValues<DecisionTypeOption>())
                        {
                            <div class="form-check form-check-inline">
                                <input class="form-check-input" type="radio"
                                       name="decisionType"
                                       id="dt_@opt"
                                       checked="@(Draft.DecisionType == opt)"
                                       @onchange="() => Edit(d => d with { DecisionType = opt })" />
                                <label class="form-check-label" for="dt_@opt">
                                    @opt.ToLabel()
                                </label>
                            </div>
                        }
                    </div>)

                    @* ── Match score ─────────────────────────────────────────
                       The position-pattern field's shape, over a facet whose rule the
                       panel does not own: the lib names FilterField.MatchScores when any
                       entry is a token its grammar rejects, the input reds itself on that
                       membership, and the same verdict closes Apply — so a
                       mistyped or retired score can never silently drop the filter the
                       user meant to set.

                       Two verdicts, not one, because the remedies differ: a malformed
                       token is retyped, while a *retired* one was spelled correctly for
                       an earlier grammar and needs its replacements named. The lib draws
                       that distinction wordlessly (MatchScoreToken.GetFault) and the
                       panel words it — the GetInvalidFields / BoardPattern.TryParse
                       division of labour, one tier finer.

                       Every token spelling on this screen — placeholder, hint, and both
                       verdicts — renders from MatchScoreToken's exported constants. The
                       panel never types 'moneyJ' as a literal: the grammar that accepts
                       the token and the copy that advertises it are one source, which is
                       the whole point of the constants being exported
                       (halheinrich/backgammon#121). *@
                    @FacetRow(FilterFacet.MatchScores, @<div class="ms-3">
                        <label class="form-label">
                            <small class="text-muted" id="facetHint_MatchScores">(comma-separated; blank = all scores)</small>
                        </label>
                        <input @attributes="@(FieldAttributes(shownFields, FilterField.MatchScores, "facetHint_MatchScores", "matchScoreFeedback"))"
                               aria-labelledby="facetToggle_MatchScores"
                               placeholder="e.g. 4a5a, 1a1a, @MatchScoreToken.DoubleMatchPoint, 1a2aC, @MatchScoreToken.MoneyWithJacoby, @MatchScoreToken.MoneyWithoutJacoby"
                               value="@Draft.MatchScoresText"
                               @oninput="e => Edit(d => d with { MatchScoresText = e.Value?.ToString() ?? string.Empty })"
                               @onblur="() => LeaveField(FilterField.MatchScores)" />
                        <div class="form-text">
                            MaNa is on-roll-anchored — M = points the player on roll needs, N = the
                            opponent — so 4a5a and 5a4a are distinct (list both to catch either
                            orientation). C suffix = Crawford game;
                            @MatchScoreToken.MoneyWithJacoby / @MatchScoreToken.MoneyWithoutJacoby =
                            a session played with / without the Jacoby rule;
                            @MatchScoreToken.DoubleMatchPoint is accepted as a spelling of 1a1a.
                        </div>
                        @* Same d-block + @if as the error-range line and for the same
                           reason — the input is a sibling here, but rendering the verdict
                           only while it applies keeps one idiom for the panel's feedback —
                           and the same held box once it has shown. Its words differ by
                           fault kind, so its space is per kind too: a kind it has shown
                           and the list no longer has keeps its place in the line, saying
                           nothing (_heldScoreFaults), and the kinds still true stay where
                           they were. The faults are read only while the line is shown:
                           asking them costs a parse plus a sweep of the list, and
                           otherwise the answer is always "none". *@
                        @if (IsLineRendered(shownFields, FilterFacet.MatchScores))
                        {
                            var scoresShown = IsLineShown(shownFields, FilterFacet.MatchScores);
                            var scoreFaults = scoresShown ? MatchScoreFaults : NoScoreFaults;
                            <div @attributes="@(FeedbackLineAttributes(scoresShown, "matchScoreFeedback"))">
                                @if (scoreFaults.Contains(MatchScoreTokenFault.Malformed)
                                     || _heldScoreFaults.Contains(MatchScoreTokenFault.Malformed))
                                {
                                    <span @attributes="@(FeedbackPartAttributes(scoreFaults.Contains(MatchScoreTokenFault.Malformed)))">
                                        Not a valid score — write MaNa like 4a5a, add C for a
                                        Crawford game, or use @MatchScoreToken.DoubleMatchPoint,
                                        @MatchScoreToken.MoneyWithJacoby or
                                        @MatchScoreToken.MoneyWithoutJacoby; fix it to apply, or
                                        clear it for no score filter.
                                    </span>
                                }
                                @if (scoreFaults.Contains(MatchScoreTokenFault.Retired)
                                     || _heldScoreFaults.Contains(MatchScoreTokenFault.Retired))
                                {
                                    <span @attributes="@(FeedbackPartAttributes(scoreFaults.Contains(MatchScoreTokenFault.Retired)))">
                                        The @MatchScoreToken.RetiredMoney token is retired — a
                                        session now names the Jacoby rule it was played under: use
                                        @(string.Join(" or ", MatchScoreToken.RetiredMoneyReplacements)),
                                        or list both to admit either.
                                    </span>
                                }
                            </div>
                        }
                    </div>)

                    @* ── Move number range ───────────────────────────────────
                       The error range's sibling, wired identically because it is
                       the same shape of control under the same division of labour:
                       the lib rules on these bounds too, GetInvalidFields names the
                       offending one, each input reds itself on its own FilterField,
                       and a misordered pair blames both while neither bound is
                       wrong alone. It reads the hoisted shownFields evaluation
                       the error range reads, and closes Apply through the same
                       verdict — no rule of the panel's own, here or
                       there. The facets differ only in the floor each bound must
                       clear (zero for an error magnitude, one for a 1-based move
                       ordinal), and the floor is the lib's to set; the panel only
                       words it. *@
                    @FacetRow(FilterFacet.MoveNumberRange, @<div class="ms-3">
                        <label class="form-label">
                            <small class="text-muted" id="facetHint_MoveNumberRange">(inclusive; blank = no bound; non-standard-start games are excluded)</small>
                        </label>
                        <div class="d-flex gap-2 align-items-center">
                            <input id="moveNumberMin"
                                   @attributes="@(FieldAttributes(shownFields, FilterField.MoveNumberMin, "facetHint_MoveNumberRange", "moveNumberFeedback"))"
                                   style="max-width:140px"
                                   aria-labelledby="facetToggle_MoveNumberRange"
                                   type="text" inputmode="numeric" placeholder="Min"
                                   value="@Draft.MoveNumberMinText"
                                   @oninput="e => Edit(d => d with { MoveNumberMinText = e.Value?.ToString() ?? string.Empty })"
                                   @onblur="() => LeaveField(FilterField.MoveNumberMin)" />
                            <span>–</span>
                            <input id="moveNumberMax"
                                   @attributes="@(FieldAttributes(shownFields, FilterField.MoveNumberMax, "facetHint_MoveNumberRange", "moveNumberFeedback"))"
                                   style="max-width:140px"
                                   aria-labelledby="facetToggle_MoveNumberRange"
                                   type="text" inputmode="numeric" placeholder="Max"
                                   value="@Draft.MoveNumberMaxText"
                                   @oninput="e => Edit(d => d with { MoveNumberMaxText = e.Value?.ToString() ?? string.Empty })"
                                   @onblur="() => LeaveField(FilterField.MoveNumberMax)" />
                        </div>
                        @* #errorRangeFeedback's shape, for the same reasons: the
                           explicit @if and d-block because the inputs sit one level
                           down inside the flex row and Bootstrap's sibling selector
                           cannot reach across, one line for both violations
                           because the user fixes either at the same two boxes, and
                           the same held box once shown — given up when this row,
                           or the container over it, closes.
                           Keyed to this facet's own two fields, never to "the lib
                           named something" — the invalid set spans facets, and a
                           line that fired on any membership would explain the move
                           numbers over a fault the user committed in another row.
                           The floor is stated here and nowhere else on this screen.
                           The hint at the head of this row's body says what the boxes
                           *mean* (which end is which, what blank does, which games the
                           facet can speak about at all); this says what they will
                           *accept*, and only while something is not accepted. The
                           error range splits its own copy on exactly that line —
                           "(inclusive; blank = no bound)" above, "zero or greater"
                           down here — so a reader who learns one facet's boxes
                           knows where to look for the other's. *@
                        @if (IsLineRendered(shownFields, FilterFacet.MoveNumberRange))
                        {
                            <div @attributes="@(FeedbackLineAttributes(IsLineShown(shownFields, FilterFacet.MoveNumberRange), "moveNumberFeedback"))">
                                Each bound must be a whole number, one or greater, and
                                Min can't be above Max — fix it to apply, or clear a
                                bound for no limit on that side.
                            </div>
                        }
                    </div>)

                    @* ── Contact type ────────────────────────────────────────── *@
                    @FacetRow(FilterFacet.ContactTypes, @<div class="ms-3">
                        <label class="form-label">
                            <small class="text-muted">(none checked = all types)</small>
                        </label>
                        <div>
                            @foreach (var ct in Enum.GetValues<ContactType>())
                            {
                                <div class="form-check form-check-inline">
                                    <input class="form-check-input" type="checkbox"
                                           id="ct_@ct"
                                           checked="@Draft.ContactTypes.Contains(ct)"
                                           @onchange="(e) => Edit(d => d.WithContactType(ct, e.Value is true))" />
                                    <label class="form-check-label" for="ct_@ct">@ct.ToLabel()</label>
                                </div>
                            }
                        </div>
                    </div>)

                    @* ── Analysis depth ──────────────────────────────────────
                       Union-of-clauses facet over BgDataTypes_Lib's AnalysisMode ×
                       AnalysisLevel: three per-mode pairs, each a mode toggle plus its own
                       level group. The panel binds the six raw-intent inputs (three bools,
                       three level sets) verbatim and derives nothing — the mapping to
                       AnalysisDepthFilter clauses (one clause per enabled toggle, empty
                       level list = any level, all toggles off = facet off, inert level
                       lists) lives only in FilterConfig.Build(); re-encoding it here would
                       duplicate the SSOT and silently drift — see this panel's Pitfalls and
                       XgFilter_Lib's Depth-facet block. Mode labels come from AnalysisMode's
                       [Description]s via EnumLabel.ToLabel, never hardcoded. Levels render
                       in Enum.GetValues declaration order (the lib's ascending-rigor order);
                       Unknown is a first-class, selectable level.

                       Each level group is an honest disclosure in the facet rows' idiom one
                       tier down — real button, aria-expanded / aria-controls, children
                       rendered only while expanded — but deliberately UNPERSISTED, unlike
                       the rows' own DisclosureKey set: while collapsed, the count badge
                       ("any" / "N selected") already carries everything the closed state
                       hides, so remembering the open state buys no information and would
                       cost three more localStorage entries. The badge reads the live
                       draft, so it is honest mid-edit and after restore, a saved
                       filter's staging, and Clear. Collapsing or expanding a group is
                       navigation, not an edit — the owner hears nothing, exactly as for
                       the row above it.

                       Unchecking a mode hides its level group but keeps the checked levels
                       in the draft (and in the committed config): the lib guarantees a level
                       list whose toggle is off is inert — no activation, no constraint — so
                       re-toggling the mode restores the user's selection instead of
                       punishing an exploratory untoggle. *@
                    @FacetRow(FilterFacet.AnalysisDepth, @<div class="ms-3">
                        <label class="form-label">
                            <small class="text-muted">(check the modes you want to see — checking more shows more; nothing checked means no depth filter)</small>
                        </label>
                        @foreach (var group in DepthGroups)
                        {
                            <div>
                                <div class="form-check">
                                    <input class="form-check-input" type="checkbox"
                                           id="md_@group.Mode"
                                           checked="@Draft.Includes(group.Mode)"
                                           @onchange="(e) => Edit(d => d.WithIncludes(group.Mode, e.Value is true))" />
                                    <label class="form-check-label" for="md_@group.Mode">@group.Mode.ToLabel()</label>
                                </div>
                                @if (Draft.Includes(group.Mode))
                                {
                                    <div class="ms-4 mb-2">
                                        <button type="button" id="lvlToggle_@group.Mode"
                                                class="btn btn-link btn-sm p-0 text-decoration-none"
                                                aria-expanded="@(group.LevelsExpanded ? "true" : "false")"
                                                aria-controls="lvl_@group.Mode"
                                                @onclick="() => group.LevelsExpanded = !group.LevelsExpanded">
                                            Analysis level
                                            @if (!group.LevelsExpanded)
                                            {
                                                var levelCount = Draft.LevelsOf(group.Mode).Count;
                                                <span id="lvlBadge_@group.Mode"
                                                      class="badge ms-1 @(levelCount > 0 ? "text-bg-primary" : "text-bg-secondary")">
                                                    @(levelCount > 0 ? $"{levelCount} selected" : "any")
                                                </span>
                                            }
                                        </button>
                                        <div id="lvl_@group.Mode">
                                            @if (group.LevelsExpanded)
                                            {
                                                <div>
                                                    @foreach (var level in Enum.GetValues<AnalysisLevel>())
                                                    {
                                                        <div class="form-check form-check-inline">
                                                            <input class="form-check-input" type="checkbox"
                                                                   id="lv_@(group.Mode)_@level"
                                                                   checked="@Draft.LevelsOf(group.Mode).Contains(level)"
                                                                   @onchange="(e) => Edit(d => d.WithLevel(group.Mode, level, e.Value is true))" />
                                                            <label class="form-check-label" for="lv_@(group.Mode)_@level">@level.ToLabel()</label>
                                                        </div>
                                                    }
                                                </div>
                                                <div class="form-text">None checked = any level. @group.LevelHint</div>
                                            }
                                        </div>
                                    </div>
                                }
                            </div>
                        }
                    </div>)

                    @* ── Dice rolls ──────────────────────────────────────────
                       One checkbox per distinct roll, enumerated from DiceRoll.All — the lib
                       owns both the 21-roll set and its ascending canonical order (11, 21, 22,
                       31, … 66, the six doubles included), so the panel imposes no local roll
                       list or sort rule. Labels are the roll's own canonical token
                       (DiceRoll.ToString → "31"), rendered verbatim: display text stays owned
                       by the type, exactly as the enum facets defer to ToLabel. (A hyphenated
                       "3-1" presentation was considered; "31" keeps the panel free of any
                       label-munging and matches the lib's token one-for-one.) Selections are
                       held raw in the draft and handed to FilterConfig.Build() verbatim —
                       the panel derives nothing. None checked = facet off (Build() skips the
                       filter); the roll set is canonical/unordered, so checking "31" admits
                       both dice orders, and cube rows (no roll) never pass an active filter. *@
                    @FacetRow(FilterFacet.DiceRolls, @<div class="ms-3">
                        <label class="form-label">
                            <small class="text-muted">(none checked = all rolls; cube decisions never match)</small>
                        </label>
                        <div>
                            @foreach (var roll in DiceRoll.All)
                            {
                                <div class="form-check form-check-inline">
                                    <input class="form-check-input" type="checkbox"
                                           id="dr_@roll"
                                           checked="@Draft.DiceRolls.Contains(roll)"
                                           @onchange="(e) => Edit(d => d.WithDiceRoll(roll, e.Value is true))" />
                                    <label class="form-check-label" for="dr_@roll">@roll</label>
                                </div>
                            }
                        </div>
                    </div>)

                    @* ── Position pattern ────────────────────────────────────
                       Per-location checker-range constraint, entered as the lib's raw bracket
                       list until the board-grid widget replaces this field in a later arc.
                       The text is the config's text, both ways (halheinrich/backgammon#269):
                       the draft's text rides into FilterConfig.PositionPattern as typed and
                       a restored or loaded config's text lands back in the draft as stored,
                       so a stored pattern a newer grammar rule refuses still shows the user
                       what they wrote. Validity is the lib's ruling like every other
                       field's: GetInvalidFields names FilterField.PositionPattern iff the
                       text is present and BoardPattern.TryParse refuses it, the input reds
                       itself on that membership, and the same verdict closes
                       Apply — so an invalid pattern can never silently drop, or silently
                       become, the filter the user meant to set. No BoardPattern is built
                       here for the config's sake; Build() parses, on the lib's side.

                       The help text below documents the lib's grammar — single-location
                       tokens over board indices and the named borne-off locations, and the
                       [a-b,min,max] range token (halheinrich/backgammon#268) — but does not
                       re-implement it: BoardPattern remains the single source of truth for
                       what parses. A wrong-signed bar or borne-off bound (say [off,,-2]), a
                       range with opposite-signed bounds or whose start is not below its end,
                       all fail TryParse and land in the same invalid-field state as any other
                       unparseable text, which is why the invalid-entry line names no single
                       cause: TryParse reports none, and a list of causes would be a second
                       copy of the lib's rules. The borne-off names render from
                       CheckerLocation's own canonical spelling, never as literals (see
                       Pitfalls); the example tokens are literal and pinned to parse. How the
                       opponent's at least n and at most n are written is Hal's ruling on
                       halheinrich/backgammon#275 (2026-09-25), stated here and in FilterHelp
                       alike with the ruling's own two examples.

                       Named by reference rather than from a label: the row's header
                       button already carries the facet's name, so aria-labelledby points
                       at it and aria-describedby points at the hint. The wrapper below is
                       a plain container now — its `for` is gone, because a label whose
                       text is the hint would have made the hint the field's name, which
                       is the lie this replaces. This was the panel's only named input
                       until halheinrich/backgammon#196 made the pair of references the
                       rule for every box the user types into; it is the
                       precedent the others follow, not a special case. *@
                    @FacetRow(FilterFacet.PositionPattern, @<div class="ms-3">
                        <label class="form-label">
                            <small class="text-muted" id="facetHint_PositionPattern">(checker counts by location or range of locations; blank = no pattern filter)</small>
                        </label>
                        <input id="positionPattern"
                               @attributes="@(FieldAttributes(shownFields, FilterField.PositionPattern, "facetHint_PositionPattern", "positionPatternFeedback"))"
                               aria-labelledby="facetToggle_PositionPattern"
                               placeholder="e.g. [6,2,] [5,,-2] [7-12,3,] [@CheckerLocation.OpponentOff,,-2]"
                               value="@Draft.PositionPatternText"
                               @oninput="e => Edit(d => d with { PositionPatternText = e.Value?.ToString() ?? string.Empty })"
                               @onblur="() => LeaveField(FilterField.PositionPattern)" />
                        <div class="form-text">
                            [location,min,max] per location; blank bound = unbounded; positive counts
                            belong to the player on roll, negative to the opponent.
                            Locations: points 1–24, bar 25 (player on roll) and bar 0 (opponent),
                            plus borne off — @CheckerLocation.PlayerOff for the player on roll and
                            @CheckerLocation.OpponentOff for the opponent. A bar or borne-off location
                            takes no bound of the other side's sign.
                            [a-b,min,max] totals one side across locations a to b (a below b, bars
                            included): the sign of the bounds picks the side, the other side's
                            checkers there are ignored, and opposite-signed bounds are refused.
                            [a-b,0,0] = empty of both sides; [a-b,,0] = none of the player on roll's;
                            [a-b,0,] = none of the opponent's.
                            For the opponent, at least n is written with the upper bound — [1-6,,-1] =
                            at least one of the opponent's on points 1–6 — and a lower bound means at
                            most n: [1-6,-1,] = at most one.
                            E.g. [6,2,] [5,,-2] [25,1,] [@CheckerLocation.OpponentOff,,-2] [@CheckerLocation.PlayerOff,10,] [7-12,3,] [13-18,,-2] [19-24,0,0].
                        </div>
                        @* The panel's one feedback idiom — shown only while it
                           applies, d-block, keyed to this facet's own field — so the
                           line's id exists exactly when the input's aria-describedby
                           names it. It used to sit in the DOM for Bootstrap's sibling
                           selector to reveal; a described-by reference to a hidden
                           element is read out regardless, which is why it moved. Once
                           shown, its box is held in place, saying nothing, until this
                           row or the container closes (halheinrich/backgammon#272). *@
                        @if (IsLineRendered(shownFields, FilterFacet.PositionPattern))
                        {
                            <div @attributes="@(FeedbackLineAttributes(IsLineShown(shownFields, FilterFacet.PositionPattern), "positionPatternFeedback"))">
                                Not a valid pattern — check each entry against the rules above; fix it to apply, or clear it for no pattern filter.
                            </div>
                        }
                    </div>)
                }
            </div>
        </div>

        @* ── Apply and Clear ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
           The panel's two gestures on one row, Apply first and "Clear filters"
           immediately to its right at the flex row's standard gap — the
           placement ruled in halheinrich/backgammon#230. Clear sat alone above
           the facet rows from halheinrich/backgammon#153 until then, in the
           seat it shared with the Hide/Show toggle; halheinrich/backgammon#193
           removed that toggle, so the seat outlived its reason and the two
           gestures now read where the user commits, left to right in the order
           they are reached for.

           The flex row keeps both buttons shrink-to-content rather than letting
           either stretch, and it keeps the disabled-reason line reading as the
           line under the buttons, which is how the help describes it.

           Apply wears the owner's gate (SPEC-filtering.md §2): a source is
           established, restoration has settled, no field is wrong, and the
           selection is not already in effect. ApplyDisabledReason explains
           the last case in the NamedEntriesPanel disabled-reason idiom (title
           + muted hint line); an invalid value already explains itself in the
           offending field's own invalid-feedback line. Hoisted into a local
           like the active-facet set above: both surfaces of the reason read
           the one evaluation.

           Clear is the full-clear gesture under an honest name, gated until a
           source is reported and restoration has settled (§4's transitions
           table; before a source it is off and writes nothing, Hal's ruling
           of 2026-10-08), with no reason line of its own: it clears
           filter values only — the draft and this setup's baseline to the
           empty selection, then remembered — never touches host state, and
           never opens or closes a row, so it claims nothing about what is on
           screen. *@
        @{ var applyDisabledReason = ApplyDisabledReason; }
        <div class="d-flex gap-2">
            <button class="btn btn-primary btn-sm"
                    disabled="@(!_snapshot.CanApply)"
                    title="@applyDisabledReason"
                    @onclick="ApplyAsync">
                Apply Filter
            </button>
            <button type="button" id="clearFilters"
                    class="btn btn-outline-secondary btn-sm"
                    disabled="@(!_snapshot.CanClear)"
                    @onclick="ClearFiltersAsync">
                Clear filters
            </button>
        </div>
        @if (applyDisabledReason is not null)
        {
            <div id="applyDisabledReason" class="form-text">@applyDisabledReason</div>
        }

    </div>
</div>

@code {
    // The app-scoped owner of the selection, the baseline and this boot's
    // restoration (SPEC-filtering.md §4), and the surface's storage seam for
    // this panel's own display preferences. Injected rather than passed: one
    // instance per app by construction, the same one the host reads its gate
    // from, so no binding can hand this panel a second owner.
    [Inject] private FilterSetup Setup { get; set; } = default!;
    [Inject] private FilterStorage Storage { get; set; } = default!;

    // The owner's latest snapshot — delivered on attach, before the first
    // render, and replaced at every change the owner publishes. Everything
    // this panel shows about the selection reads it.
    private FilterSetupSnapshot _snapshot = default!;
    private IDisposable? _attachment;

    // The selection on screen.
    private FilterDraft Draft => _snapshot.Draft;

    protected override void OnInitialized() => _attachment = Setup.Attach(OnSetupChanged);

    // Each snapshot becomes this panel's, and a render follows. The first
    // arrives inside Attach, before the first render, so it needs none.
    private void OnSetupChanged(FilterSetupSnapshot snapshot)
    {
        var previous = _snapshot;
        _snapshot = snapshot;

        // What each box shows of its verdict (halheinrich/backgammon#272). A
        // mount shows every wrong value at once — none of them was typed
        // here. After that, a draft this panel's own typing changed keeps
        // only the reveals still true, so a correction clears its message at
        // once and a fresh error waits for the user to leave the box; a draft
        // changed any other way — restored, staged, cleared — shows every
        // wrong value at once, as a value that arrives rather than one being
        // typed.
        if (previous is null || !snapshot.Draft.Equals(previous.Draft))
        {
            if (previous is not null && _typing)
            {
                _revealed.IntersectWith(snapshot.InvalidFields);
            }
            else
            {
                _revealed = [.. snapshot.InvalidFields];
            }
        }

        // What is shown now holds its space from here on; a correction that
        // took a message away keeps the box it was in (see _heldLines).
        HoldShownLines();

        if (previous is not null)
        {
            _ = InvokeAsync(StateHasChanged);
        }
    }

    /// <summary>Stops observing the owner; anything the owner is doing carries on without this panel.</summary>
    public void Dispose() => _attachment?.Dispose();

    /// <summary>
    /// What the container's toggle is called while the rows are folded away
    /// behind it — the words on the control, and the accessible name that
    /// follows from them.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// <c>internal</c> for <see cref="FilterStorage.ConfigKey"/>'s reason and no other: it
    /// is how <see cref="FilterHelp"/> names this control in prose without
    /// retyping it, exactly as that block renders a facet heading from the
    /// lib's <c>ToLabel()</c> rather than spelling the facet out. Deliberately
    /// not <c>public</c> — a consumer must never name this panel's chrome,
    /// which is why the copy that does is producer-owned and lives here.
    /// </remarks>
    internal const string MoreFiltersFoldedLabel = "More filters";

    /// <summary>
    /// What the same toggle is called while the rows are showing — the
    /// gesture it now offers, not the state the user is already looking at
    /// (<c>halheinrich/backgammon#239</c>). <see cref="MoreFiltersFoldedLabel"/>'s
    /// twin in every other respect, including why it is <c>internal</c>.
    /// </summary>
    internal const string MoreFiltersExpandedLabel = "Fewer filters";

    /// <summary>
    /// Which of the two the toggle wears right now — the rule that picks
    /// between them, owned here with the words themselves so the panel's
    /// markup and <see cref="FilterHelp"/>'s prose cannot disagree about what
    /// the control is called in either state. <c>private</c> because only this
    /// panel renders a label: the help names both and leaves the choosing
    /// alone, and no test needs the rule when it can read the button.
    /// </summary>
    private string MoreFiltersLabel =>
        _moreFiltersExpanded ? MoreFiltersExpandedLabel : MoreFiltersFoldedLabel;

    // Depth facet — raw user intent as three per-mode pairs, held in the
    // draft verbatim and handed to FilterConfig.Build(), which owns the
    // clause-union derivation. The panel never maps toggles and level lists
    // to clauses itself (that SSOT lives in the lib; see the Analysis-depth
    // markup comment). A group here is the rendering of one pair: its mode,
    // its hint copy, and its level disclosure's session-only open/closed
    // state — navigation, never persisted (see the markup comment for why, in
    // contrast to DisclosureKey). The pair's values are the draft's.
    private sealed class DepthModeGroup
    {
        public required AnalysisMode Mode { get; init; }

        // Mode-specific hint line rendered after the shared "None checked =
        // any level." sentence — panel-owned copy explaining what "level"
        // means for this mode.
        public required string LevelHint { get; init; }

        public bool LevelsExpanded { get; set; }
    }

    private readonly DepthModeGroup _evaluations = new()
    {
        Mode = AnalysisMode.Evaluation,
        LevelHint = "The level is the evaluation's own.",
    };

    private readonly DepthModeGroup _rollouts = new()
    {
        Mode = AnalysisMode.Rollout,
        LevelHint = "The level is the inner level the rollout's games were played at — "
            + "checker plays never carry XG Roller-family inner levels.",
    };

    private readonly DepthModeGroup _bookRollouts = new()
    {
        Mode = AnalysisMode.BookRollout,
        LevelHint = "The level is the inner level from the opening-book entry, "
            + "when the book recorded it.",
    };

    // Render order = AnalysisMode declaration order (Evaluation, Rollout,
    // BookRollout). A property rather than a field because field initializers
    // cannot reference the instance fields above.
    private IReadOnlyList<DepthModeGroup> DepthGroups => [_evaluations, _rollouts, _bookRollouts];

    // The facets that carry a row, in render order: FilterFacet declaration
    // order minus ErrorRange, which is always visible above the rows, and
    // minus the two UI-shelved facets (PositionTypes, PlayTypes), which an old
    // saved config can still activate but which this panel offers no control
    // for. It is the vocabulary of the persisted expanded-row set — a stored
    // name outside it is corruption, not a row — and the order the set is
    // written in.
    //
    // Deliberately NOT the source the markup loops over: each row's body is
    // its own markup, so the eight FacetRow calls and this list are two
    // statements of the same membership. FilterPanelTests pins them equal
    // (the rendered #facetToggle_* ids against this list) rather than letting
    // one silently outlive the other — a pin that is only meaningful because
    // the two are genuinely separate.
    private static readonly FilterFacet[] RowFacets =
    [
        FilterFacet.Players,
        FilterFacet.DecisionType,
        FilterFacet.MatchScores,
        FilterFacet.MoveNumberRange,
        FilterFacet.ContactTypes,
        FilterFacet.AnalysisDepth,
        FilterFacet.DiceRolls,
        FilterFacet.PositionPattern,
    ];

    // The stored name → row lookup, built once from RowFacets. Restore reads
    // names through this rather than Enum.TryParse: TryParse also accepts a
    // numeric token ("0" would arrive as Players) and every FilterFacet member,
    // row or not, so it would honour two kinds of value this panel never writes.
    private static readonly IReadOnlyDictionary<string, FilterFacet> RowFacetsByName =
        RowFacets.ToDictionary(f => f.ToString(), StringComparer.Ordinal);

    // Which rows are open. Default: none — every row starts collapsed, the
    // posture the hierarchy ruling asks for. _disclosureTouched is true once
    // the user has opened or closed a row this mount, checked before and after
    // the restore's await so a restore completing late can never clobber a
    // fresh user choice. Blazor's single-threaded sync context makes the
    // segments between awaits atomic, so a plain bool is a sufficient guard.
    private HashSet<FilterFacet> _expandedFacets = [];
    private bool _disclosureTouched;

    // Whether the container over those rows is open. Default: folded — the
    // same posture one tier up, and the state the container exists to make the
    // panel's resting one. _moreFiltersTouched is _disclosureTouched's twin,
    // and it is a second flag rather than a share of that one because the two
    // preferences restore from two keys: a user gesture on one must not
    // silence the other's late restore.
    private bool _moreFiltersExpanded;
    private bool _moreFiltersTouched;

    protected override async Task OnAfterRenderAsync(bool firstRender)
    {
        if (!firstRender) return;

        // The owner's restoration runs once per boot: this starts it on the
        // first mount and finds it settled, or still in flight, on every later
        // one. The display preferences are this mount's own. The restoration is
        // awaited last only so a failure that is not a refusal surfaces here,
        // in the lifecycle, rather than being lost.
        try
        {
            var restoration = Setup.RestoreAsync();
            await RestoreMoreFiltersAsync();
            await RestoreDisclosureAsync();
            await restoration;
            _mountRestored.TrySetResult();

            // The marker moves from Pending to the outcome here, and only
            // here: this render is the one the settled restores produce.
            StateHasChanged();
        }
        catch (Exception failure)
        {
            _mountRestored.TrySetException(failure);
            throw;
        }
    }

    // Completes when this mount's restores have settled — its two display
    // preferences and the boot's restoration of the selection. It is the one
    // observation the restores' own continuation produces: a late restore
    // that rightly yields to a gesture renders nothing, so no render can
    // prove it ran.
    private readonly TaskCompletionSource _mountRestored = new(TaskCreationOptions.RunContinuationsAsynchronously);

    /// <summary>Completes when this mount's restores have settled (see the field above).</summary>
    internal Task MountRestored => _mountRestored.Task;

    /// <summary>
    /// The attribute on the panel's root that reports this boot's restoration
    /// of the selection to a host's browser test (halheinrich/backgammon#346):
    /// <see cref="FilterRestoration.Pending"/> until this mount has applied
    /// everything it restores — its two display preferences and the boot's
    /// restoration of the selection — and then the selection's outcome, by its
    /// <see cref="FilterRestoration"/> member name. It is observable on
    /// failure too (<c>Refused</c>, <c>Unreadable</c>), so nothing that waits
    /// on it waits forever, and it says nothing about whether Apply or Run is
    /// permitted. A test waits for it to leave Pending before it acts, which is
    /// what lets it click the container's toggle without racing the mount's
    /// own restore of it.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// <c>internal</c>: a host's test never spells it — the producer's test
    /// support (<c>XgFilter_Razor.Testing.FilterRestorationMarker</c>) builds
    /// the selectors from it, the storage keys' posture for a DOM fact. Host
    /// code reads the outcome from <see cref="FilterSetupSnapshot.Restoration"/>.
    /// </remarks>
    internal const string RestorationAttribute = "data-filter-restoration";

    // The marker's one attribute, as the root's splat. Pending until this
    // mount's restores have settled, whatever the boot's outcome already is,
    // so a remount reports its own settling too.
    private IReadOnlyDictionary<string, object> RestorationMarker => new Dictionary<string, object>(StringComparer.Ordinal)
    {
        [RestorationAttribute] = (_mountRestored.Task.IsCompletedSuccessfully
            ? _snapshot.Restoration
            : FilterRestoration.Pending).ToString(),
    };

    // The container's restore, RestoreDisclosureAsync's shape over one bit:
    // re-checking its own touched flag after the await so a gesture that
    // landed while the read was in flight wins. Tolerant like the rows':
    // anything bool.TryParse refuses — nothing stored and a refused read
    // included — leaves the container folded.
    private async Task RestoreMoreFiltersAsync()
    {
        if (_moreFiltersTouched) return;

        var read = await Storage.ReadAsync(FilterStorage.MoreFiltersKey);

        if (_moreFiltersTouched) return;

        if (bool.TryParse(read.Value, out var open))
        {
            _moreFiltersExpanded = open;
            HoldShownLines();
            StateHasChanged();
        }
    }

    // Which rows are open belongs to the user, so nothing that stages filter
    // values — a saved filter loaded, the selection restored — suppresses
    // restoring them.
    private async Task RestoreDisclosureAsync()
    {
        if (_disclosureTouched) return;

        var read = await Storage.ReadAsync(FilterStorage.DisclosureKey);

        // Re-check after the await: a row toggled while the read was in
        // flight is a fresh user choice and must win.
        if (_disclosureTouched) return;

        if (TryParseExpandedFacets(read.Value, out var restored))
        {
            _expandedFacets = restored;
            HoldShownLines();
            StateHasChanged();
        }
    }

    // Tolerant restore, all-or-nothing: the stored value must be a JSON array
    // of names this panel could itself have written, and one element that is
    // not discards the whole preference rather than salvaging the rest. The
    // strict reading is the honest one — the panel only ever writes row names,
    // so anything else is corruption or a hand edit, and half-honouring it
    // would restore a set the user never chose. Returning false leaves the
    // caller's default (every row collapsed) in place.
    private static bool TryParseExpandedFacets(
        string? stored, [NotNullWhen(true)] out HashSet<FilterFacet>? facets)
    {
        facets = null;

        if (string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(stored)) return false;

        // Declared string?[] although the metadata is string[]'s: the two are
        // one runtime type, and a stored [null] really does put a null in it,
        // so the element nullability the check below relies on is stated
        // rather than assumed away.
        string?[]? names;
        try
        {
            names = JsonSerializer.Deserialize(stored, XgFilterRazorJsonContext.Default.StringArray);
        }
        catch (JsonException)
        {
            return false;
        }

        if (names is null) return false;

        var parsed = new HashSet<FilterFacet>();
        foreach (var name in names)
        {
            if (name is null || !RowFacetsByName.TryGetValue(name, out var facet)) return false;
            parsed.Add(facet);
        }

        facets = parsed;
        return true;
    }

    // Why Apply is disabled — the NamedEntriesPanel disabled-reason idiom
    // (button title plus a muted hint line), except that here the panel knows
    // its own reason instead of being told it by the host. Covers only the
    // nothing-to-apply cases, of which there are two: the empty selection,
    // which is in effect without Apply (halheinrich/backgammon#266), and a
    // selection already applied. The empty one says so first, since "already
    // applied" would be false of a selection nobody applied. An invalid value
    // already explains itself in its own field's invalid-feedback line, and
    // re-stating it here would be a second encoding of the same rule. Null
    // while Apply is enabled, so no stale explanation lingers.
    private string? ApplyDisabledReason =>
        _snapshot.IsTheEmptySelectionInEffect
            ? "Nothing to apply — no filter is set, so every decision is included."
            : _snapshot.IsAlreadyApplied
                ? "No changes to apply — this filter is already applied."
                : null;

    // The owner guards both commits on the gate it publishes, so a dispatch
    // that ignores the disabled attribute cannot re-commit an unchanged
    // selection or clear before restoration settles. The click's task is the
    // owner's: it completes when the commit's write has been answered.
    private Task ApplyAsync() => Setup.ApplyAsync();

    // "Clear filters" — the full-clear gesture, the owner's: draft and
    // baseline to the empty selection, then remembered. Filter values only:
    // the panel has no path to host state, and which rows are open is the
    // user's choice, not this gesture's.
    private Task ClearFiltersAsync() => Setup.ClearAsync();

    // Flip one row + persist the whole set. Not an edit gesture: the owner
    // hears nothing and the selection is not written — the choice is
    // remembered immediately under its own key, independent of Apply. A
    // refused write keeps the row as the user left it for this mount; the
    // seam has told the host.
    // Closing a row gives up its feedback line's held space; opening one
    // holds a line it shows at once (see _heldLines).
    private async Task ToggleFacetAsync(FilterFacet facet)
    {
        _disclosureTouched = true;
        if (_expandedFacets.Remove(facet))
        {
            ReleaseLine(facet);
        }
        else
        {
            _expandedFacets.Add(facet);
            HoldShownLines();
        }

        await Storage.WriteAsync(FilterStorage.DisclosureKey, SerializeOpenRows(_expandedFacets));
    }

    // Fold or unfold the container + persist the one bit, ToggleFacetAsync's
    // shape and for the same reasons: not an edit gesture, so the owner hears
    // nothing and the selection is not written, and the choice is remembered
    // immediately under its own key rather than at Apply.
    // Folding the container folds every row inside it away, so their lines
    // give up their held space; unfolding holds what they show at once. The
    // error range is outside the container and keeps its own.
    private async Task ToggleMoreFiltersAsync()
    {
        _moreFiltersTouched = true;
        _moreFiltersExpanded = !_moreFiltersExpanded;
        if (_moreFiltersExpanded)
        {
            HoldShownLines();
        }
        else
        {
            foreach (var line in FeedbackLines.Where(line => line != FilterFacet.ErrorRange))
            {
                ReleaseLine(line);
            }
        }

        await Storage.WriteAsync(FilterStorage.MoreFiltersKey, SerializeFold(_moreFiltersExpanded));
    }

    // The container's state as stored: the two literals are the whole wire
    // format — see FilterStorage.MoreFiltersKey. Internal, with
    // SerializeOpenRows, so the producer's test support states a toggle's
    // write for a host's test in this panel's own spelling rather than a copy.
    internal static string SerializeFold(bool open) => open ? "true" : "false";

    // The open rows as the stored JSON array. Written in RowFacets order rather
    // than the set's own enumeration order, so one arrangement of open rows has
    // one spelling on disk: a hash-ordered array would differ run to run for
    // the same user choice and make the stored value unreadable to a person
    // inspecting it in devtools, which the help invites them to do.
    //
    // Both directions go through XgFilterRazorJsonContext's source-generated
    // metadata, never a reflection-bound JsonSerializer overload: this
    // assembly is trimmable, and a trimmed host (BgQuiz) refuses the
    // reflection path at publish — halheinrich/backgammon#193 shipped it once
    // and broke that publish. The trim analyzer in this project's own build
    // now refuses it first, as IL2026 under TreatWarningsAsErrors.
    internal static string SerializeOpenRows(IEnumerable<FilterFacet> openRows)
    {
        var open = openRows.ToHashSet();
        return JsonSerializer.Serialize(
            RowFacets.Where(open.Contains).Select(f => f.ToString()).ToArray(),
            XgFilterRazorJsonContext.Default.StringArray);
    }

    // Which facets currently hold active filters — the presence half of every
    // row's badge. Judged by the lib's activation predicates via
    // GetActiveFacets() on the draft's parsed config, the same build path Apply
    // commits through, never by re-inspecting a box or a config field here —
    // joined, by the draft, by any box whose text its field cannot be, which
    // is a criterion the user meant rather than none (FilterDraft.ActiveFacets).
    // Recomputed per render; the cost is a parse plus predicate sweep,
    // accepted over caching.
    private IReadOnlySet<FilterFacet> ActiveFacets => _snapshot.ActiveFacets;

    // The words on an active row's badge — the panel's display concern, and
    // the only half of the badge that is. It says how much the collapsed row
    // is hiding in the terms that facet is chosen in: a free-text or bounds
    // facet is simply "set" (a count of boxes typed in would tell the user
    // nothing they wanted), while a facet chosen by ticking options reports how
    // many are ticked. Never asked of an inactive facet: the caller renders no
    // badge at all in that state, which is why no member here answers "off".
    private string FacetBadgeText(FilterFacet facet) => facet switch
    {
        FilterFacet.Players or FilterFacet.MatchScores
            or FilterFacet.MoveNumberRange or FilterFacet.PositionPattern => "set",

        // A radio group: exactly one option is ever chosen, and the facet is
        // active only while that choice is not the inactive default — so the
        // count is one by construction. A literal because that is what it is,
        // not a number this panel could arrive at differently.
        FilterFacet.DecisionType => "1 selected",

        FilterFacet.ContactTypes => $"{Draft.ContactTypes.Count} selected",
        FilterFacet.AnalysisDepth => $"{DepthGroups.Count(g => Draft.Includes(g.Mode))} selected",
        FilterFacet.DiceRolls => $"{Draft.DiceRolls.Count} selected",

        _ => throw new ArgumentOutOfRangeException(
            nameof(facet), facet, "Not a facet this panel gives a row."),
    };

    // One edit gesture, the owner's: the draft it produces becomes the
    // draft, applied to whatever the draft is when it arrives. Every
    // control's handler comes through here, so no control can change the
    // selection behind the owner's back. It is also how this panel knows a
    // change is its user typing, not a value arriving: the owner publishes
    // inside the call, so the snapshot it delivers meanwhile is this
    // gesture's (see OnSetupChanged).
    private void Edit(Func<FilterDraft, FilterDraft> edit)
    {
        _typing = true;
        try
        {
            Setup.Edit(edit);
        }
        finally
        {
            _typing = false;
        }
    }

    // True only while this panel's own Edit is in the owner's hands.
    private bool _typing;

    // The wrong fields whose mark and message this panel shows. Panel state,
    // per mount, because it is presentation: a remount starts from "every
    // wrong value is shown", which is what a value not typed in this mount
    // deserves. Never wider than the verdict: ShownFields intersects it.
    private HashSet<FilterField> _revealed = [];

    // The user left a box: reveal what is wrong in the group of fields its
    // message speaks for — the two bounds of a range share one line, and a
    // misordered pair blames both — and nothing elsewhere.
    private void LeaveField(FilterField field)
    {
        foreach (var member in FieldsOf(LineOf(field)))
        {
            if (_snapshot.InvalidFields.Contains(member))
            {
                _revealed.Add(member);
            }
        }

        HoldShownLines();
    }

    // ── Feedback lines and the space they hold ───────────────────────────────
    //
    // The panel's four feedback lines, each named by the facet it speaks for:
    // the error range's and the move numbers' (one line per pair of bounds),
    // the match scores' and the position pattern's.
    private static readonly FilterFacet[] FeedbackLines =
    [
        FilterFacet.ErrorRange,
        FilterFacet.MatchScores,
        FilterFacet.MoveNumberRange,
        FilterFacet.PositionPattern,
    ];

    // The line a field's message is on.
    private static FilterFacet LineOf(FilterField field) => field switch
    {
        FilterField.ErrorMin or FilterField.ErrorMax => FilterFacet.ErrorRange,
        FilterField.MoveNumberMin or FilterField.MoveNumberMax => FilterFacet.MoveNumberRange,
        FilterField.MatchScores => FilterFacet.MatchScores,
        FilterField.PositionPattern => FilterFacet.PositionPattern,
        _ => throw new ArgumentOutOfRangeException(nameof(field), field, "Not a field this panel words a verdict for."),
    };

    // The fields one feedback line speaks for.
    private static FilterField[] FieldsOf(FilterFacet line) => line switch
    {
        FilterFacet.ErrorRange => [FilterField.ErrorMin, FilterField.ErrorMax],
        FilterFacet.MoveNumberRange => [FilterField.MoveNumberMin, FilterField.MoveNumberMax],
        FilterFacet.MatchScores => [FilterField.MatchScores],
        FilterFacet.PositionPattern => [FilterField.PositionPattern],
        _ => throw new ArgumentOutOfRangeException(nameof(line), line, "Not a facet with a feedback line."),
    };

    // Whether a line's message is shown: any field it speaks for is.
    private static bool IsLineShown(IReadOnlySet<FilterField> shownFields, FilterFacet line) =>
        FieldsOf(line).Any(shownFields.Contains);

    // Whether a line's group is on screen: the error range always is; a row's
    // only while the More filters container and the row are both open — the
    // markup's own condition for rendering the row's body.
    private bool IsGroupVisible(FilterFacet line) =>
        line == FilterFacet.ErrorRange || (_moreFiltersExpanded && _expandedFacets.Contains(line));

    // The lines whose space is held (halheinrich/backgammon#272; Hal's ruling
    // of 2026-10-08). A line, once shown on screen, keeps its space while its
    // group is visible. A corrected message clears at once, and so does its
    // announcement, but the box keeps its place and size, so nothing below it
    // moves while the user types. The space goes only when the group folds
    // away — its row closes, or the More filters container over it does — or
    // when this panel unmounts. For the always-visible error range, that is
    // unmount. Leaving a box, Apply, Clear, a staged saved filter and a restore
    // never release it: a blur can move the clicked button between press and
    // release, and a restore can finish while the user is typing.
    //
    // Recorded at the state changes that can show a line (HoldShownLines),
    // not after a render: under a server host, OnAfterRender waits for the
    // browser's acknowledgement, and a keystroke handled in between would
    // find a line shown but not yet held.
    private readonly HashSet<FilterFacet> _heldLines = [];

    // The match-score fault kinds its line has shown since its space was
    // held. Its words differ by kind, so its space is the space of those
    // words: a kind the list no longer has keeps its place, saying nothing.
    private readonly HashSet<MatchScoreTokenFault> _heldScoreFaults = [];

    // Hold every line now shown in a visible group. Called wherever what is
    // shown or what is visible can grow: a snapshot, a reveal, a group opened
    // by the user or by a restored preference.
    private void HoldShownLines()
    {
        var shownFields = ShownFields;
        foreach (var line in FeedbackLines)
        {
            if (!IsGroupVisible(line) || !IsLineShown(shownFields, line)) continue;

            _heldLines.Add(line);
            if (line == FilterFacet.MatchScores)
            {
                _heldScoreFaults.UnionWith(MatchScoreFaults);
            }
        }
    }

    // A group folded away: its line gives its space up.
    private void ReleaseLine(FilterFacet line)
    {
        _heldLines.Remove(line);
        if (line == FilterFacet.MatchScores)
        {
            _heldScoreFaults.Clear();
        }
    }

    // Whether a line renders at all: shown, or holding its space.
    private bool IsLineRendered(IReadOnlySet<FilterField> shownFields, FilterFacet line) =>
        IsLineShown(shownFields, line) || _heldLines.Contains(line);

    /// <summary>
    /// The attribute a held feedback line carries in place of its id, naming
    /// the line whose space it holds — the test suite's handle on a box that
    /// says nothing.
    /// </summary>
    internal const string HeldLineAttribute = "data-held-for";

    // A feedback line's box. Shown, it carries the id its inputs'
    // aria-describedby names, so it is announced. Held, it is the same box in
    // the same place at the same size, saying nothing: no id, so nothing
    // names it; invisible and aria-hidden; marked with the line it holds.
    private static IReadOnlyDictionary<string, object> FeedbackLineAttributes(bool shown, string id) => shown
        ? new Dictionary<string, object>(StringComparer.Ordinal)
        {
            ["id"] = id,
            ["class"] = "invalid-feedback d-block",
        }
        : new Dictionary<string, object>(StringComparer.Ordinal)
        {
            ["class"] = "invalid-feedback d-block invisible",
            ["aria-hidden"] = "true",
            [HeldLineAttribute] = id,
        };

    // A part of a shown line that is not true now — a match-score fault kind
    // the line has shown and the list no longer has — keeps its place, saying
    // nothing; a part that is true carries nothing extra.
    private static IReadOnlyDictionary<string, object> FeedbackPartAttributes(bool shown) => shown
        ? new Dictionary<string, object>(StringComparer.Ordinal)
        : new Dictionary<string, object>(StringComparer.Ordinal)
        {
            ["class"] = "invisible",
            ["aria-hidden"] = "true",
        };

    private static readonly IReadOnlySet<MatchScoreTokenFault> NoScoreFaults = new HashSet<MatchScoreTokenFault>();

    // What the boxes show: the verdict, as far as it is revealed. The verdict
    // is the snapshot's — the lib's on the bounds, the score tokens and the
    // pattern text, asked of the same parsed config Apply commits, so what the
    // panel reds and what Build() would throw on are one answer; and the
    // draft's own on a box whose text its field cannot be at all
    // (FilterDraft.InvalidFields). Never wider than the verdict, so a
    // corrected box clears at once whatever was revealed.
    private IReadOnlySet<FilterField> ShownFields =>
        _snapshot.InvalidFields.Where(_revealed.Contains).ToHashSet();

    // How an input wears the verdict — the one place a field's validity is
    // rendered (halheinrich/backgammon#270), splatted onto every box on the
    // panel that bears a verdict, given the fields whose verdict is shown
    // (ShownFields, halheinrich/backgammon#272). Shown: the is-invalid class, aria-invalid, and
    // aria-describedby naming the field's hint and its feedback line, whose
    // id every site gives it exactly while the verdict is shown
    // (FeedbackLineAttributes), so the reference never names an element that
    // is not there, or a box that is only holding its place. Not shown: the
    // class alone and the hint alone — nothing says "invalid" in any form,
    // and nothing points at a feedback line. Only the attributes that vary
    // with validity, plus the class they ride in, so a site keeps its own id,
    // name reference and type beside the splat. Attribution stays the lib's:
    // the field asked for is the one the lib names, never a facet-wide red.
    private static IReadOnlyDictionary<string, object> FieldAttributes(
        IReadOnlySet<FilterField> shownFields, FilterField field, string hintId, string feedbackId)
    {
        var invalid = shownFields.Contains(field);
        var attributes = new Dictionary<string, object>(StringComparer.Ordinal)
        {
            ["class"] = invalid ? "form-control is-invalid" : "form-control",
            ["aria-describedby"] = invalid ? $"{hintId} {feedbackId}" : hintId,
        };

        if (invalid)
        {
            attributes["aria-invalid"] = "true";
        }

        return attributes;
    }

    // The per-token detail behind FilterField.MatchScores, which names the
    // list without saying what is wrong in it. Asked of the grammar itself
    // (MatchScoreToken.GetFault) — the same rule GetInvalidFields swept over
    // the list, so the field the lib named and the verdict the panel words
    // cannot disagree — and read off the same parsed config every other
    // verdict here reads. Distinct faults only: the line explains a kind of
    // mistake once, however many entries made it.
    private IReadOnlySet<MatchScoreTokenFault> MatchScoreFaults =>
        Draft.ToConfig().MatchScores
                     .Select(MatchScoreToken.GetFault)
                     .Where(f => f != MatchScoreTokenFault.None)
                     .ToHashSet();
}
